WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.524 --> 00:00:10.040
- Could you talk to us a little bit more

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:11.970
about your songwriting process,

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:15.370
like could you take us
through one of your songs,

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:16.610
the lyrics of one of your songs,

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:18.930
and tell us about what
your thought process was

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:20.380
as you were going through it?

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:29.181
- Um, yeah sure.

00:00:29.181 --> 00:00:32.928
(audience laughing)

00:00:32.928 --> 00:00:34.680
Here's a good one

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:38.660
to talk about lyrically.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:40.263
The song wasn't a hit,

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:43.301
but I really liked it.

00:00:43.301 --> 00:00:47.760
The song is called "Darling Lorraine",

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.493
and it's on an album
called You're the One.

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:53.950
Well first of all, it's like something

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:55.657
that is like my own private joke.

00:00:55.657 --> 00:00:59.350
"Darling Lorraine" was a
doo-wop song from way way back,

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:04.103
so I often have little quotes
of doo-wop things anyway.

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:07.973
But I have a musical,

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:10.730
you know, setting,

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:12.980
and now I'm writing lyrics.

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:16.560
So the opening lines of
songs are really crucial,

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.570
very important.

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:20.310
I try to make the opening lines,

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:22.853
in my mind, to have this angle,

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:28.220
so that I can, as opposed to,

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:30.530
now that's the end of the second verse

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:33.470
and you have nothing more to say

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:37.810
other than to repeat the
first and second verse

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:38.863
in some other form.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:41.687
So this song "Darling Lorraine",

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:45.020
I started it off with

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.660
the first time I saw
her I couldn't be sure.

00:01:52.477 --> 00:01:53.827
I don't know what that was.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.880
But the sin of impatience,

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.713
which was like a little note
that I'd written to myself.

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:04.310
I put that in there.

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:06.190
But the sin of impatience said

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:08.183
she's just what you're looking for.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:11.330
Okay.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:14.230
So I walked right up to her,

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:15.143
cliché,

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:20.370
and with the part of me that talks,

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:21.203
little joke,

00:02:22.042 --> 00:02:23.650
(audience laughing)

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:26.790
I introduced myself as Frank,

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:29.370
another joke because I
know the guy's a liar,

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:31.448
he's not Frank at all.

00:02:31.448 --> 00:02:33.840
(audience laughing)

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:36.400
From New York, New York,

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:38.487
another joke 'cause people
from New York never say,

00:02:38.487 --> 00:02:40.257
"I'm from New York, New York."

00:02:40.257 --> 00:02:41.090
(audience laughing)

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:42.510
"I'm from Syracuse, New York,"

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:44.960
but you don't say, "I'm
from New York, New York."

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:46.770
- [Emma] It's true.

00:02:48.431 --> 00:02:50.931
- So I introduce as Frank
from New York, New York.

00:02:52.208 --> 00:02:55.130
She's so hot, she's so cool.

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:59.863
I'm not, I'm just a fool in
love with Darling Lorraine.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:02.763
Cliché after cliché,

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:06.630
but I've introduced the characters,

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:10.453
Frank and Darling Lorraine.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.536
Okay, let's see, the next verse.

00:03:16.536 --> 00:03:17.369
(humming)

00:03:17.369 --> 00:03:20.040
Oh yeah, so now I begin the next verse.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:23.120
All my life I've been a wanderer

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:26.240
and I think, you're not gonna get away

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:28.183
with another verse of clichés.

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:30.060
(audience laughing)

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:32.900
So I immediately make a turn and I say

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:35.470
All my life I've been a wanderer.

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:38.430
Not really, I mostly lived
near my parents' home.

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:39.849
Anyway...

00:03:39.849 --> 00:03:42.400
(audience laughing)

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:46.100
Anyway, Lorraine and I get married,

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:48.340
and the usual marriage stuff.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:49.910
Boom, now we're moving faster.

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:51.580
I'm not even getting
into like what going on.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:54.210
I did the usual marriage stuff,

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:56.890
and then one day she says to me

00:03:56.890 --> 00:04:00.803
from out of the blue,
"Frank, I've had enough.

00:04:02.647 --> 00:04:06.097
"Romance is a heartbreaker,

00:04:06.097 --> 00:04:09.370
"and I'm not meant to be a homemaker.

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:13.107
I'm tired of being Darling Lorraine."

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:17.440
Okay, good.

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:19.575
Conflict here now.

00:04:19.575 --> 00:04:20.710
(audience laughing)

00:04:20.710 --> 00:04:22.423
What does Frank have to say?

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:27.790
What, you don't love me anymore?

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:31.210
What, you're walking out the door?

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:33.773
What, you don't like the way I chew?

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:36.957
At which point my wife Edie said,

00:04:36.957 --> 00:04:39.360
"You can't say that because
people will know that it's us."

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:40.322
I said no.

00:04:40.322 --> 00:04:43.405
(audience laughing)

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:48.993
That's every guy.

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:52.190
You don't like the way I chew?

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:54.860
Hey, let me tell you.

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:57.040
You're not the woman that I wed.

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:58.750
You say you're depressed, but you're not.

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:00.810
You just like to stay in bed.

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:03.294
Really, a guy with a lot of insight.

00:05:03.294 --> 00:05:05.010
(audience laughing)

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:06.260
She likes to stay in bed.

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:09.527
And I don't need you, darling Lorraine.

00:05:09.527 --> 00:05:11.283
Lorraine.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:13.803
Lorraine.

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:17.263
I long for your love.

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:23.200
So now the fight turns into he just,

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:25.540
you know, he admits the truth.

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:29.450
Now he's kind of vulnerable,

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:30.820
so the next thing he does

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:33.760
is completely change the subject.

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:36.180
Financially speaking,

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:38.570
(audience laughing)

00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:40.103
I guess I'm a washout.

00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:45.530
Everything is buy and
sell and sell and buy,

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:48.870
and that's what the
whole thing is all about.

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:51.120
If it had not been for Lorraine,

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:53.253
I'd have left here long ago.

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:56.300
I should have been a musician.

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:57.781
Inside joke.

00:05:57.781 --> 00:05:58.750
(audience laughing)

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:00.420
I should have been a musician.

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:01.803
I love the piano.

00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:04.483
But she's so...

00:06:06.290 --> 00:06:07.510
something, forgot.

00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:09.600
She's so, she's so...

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:12.220
She's so light.

00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:13.963
She's so free.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:18.130
I'm tight, but that's me.

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:21.433
But I feel so good with Darling Lorraine.

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:25.120
Okay, then the next part of the song

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:27.370
that I wrote was in a different key

00:06:27.370 --> 00:06:29.880
and the changes, it was so sweet.

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:31.978
It was almost sickening.

00:06:31.978 --> 00:06:32.860
(audience laughing)

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:36.483
I said what context,

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:42.060
what words would fit into
this context of so sweet?

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:45.810
So I said on Christmas morning

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:52.433
Frank awakes to find Lorraine
has made a stack of pancakes.

00:06:53.990 --> 00:06:55.330
They watch the television.

00:06:55.330 --> 00:06:58.585
I like that stack of
pancakes, I don't know why.

00:06:58.585 --> 00:07:00.270
(audience laughing)

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:02.140
Sounded good to me.

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:03.680
(audience laughing)

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:07.280
They watched the television,
husband and wife,

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:10.993
all afternoon, It's A Wonderful Life.

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:16.100
It's a pun, a pun on the Frank Capra movie

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:19.760
and also on their life.

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:21.113
It's A Wonderful Life.

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:27.713
Then, what, you don't love me anymore?

00:07:27.713 --> 00:07:31.730
What, you're walking out the door?

00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:34.810
This is like a repeat of
a chorus or something.

00:07:34.810 --> 00:07:36.950
What, you don't like the way I chew?

00:07:36.950 --> 00:07:40.070
Hey, let me tell you.

00:07:40.070 --> 00:07:41.743
You're not the woman that I wed.

00:07:41.743 --> 00:07:45.350
Gimme my robe, I'm going back to bed.

00:07:45.350 --> 00:07:47.593
I'm sick to death of you Lorraine.

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:50.810
And as soon as I wrote that I said

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.343
oh my God, she's gonna die.

00:07:58.270 --> 00:07:59.230
Then...

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:05.820
Lorraine, Lorraine.

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:08.230
Her hands like wood.

00:08:08.230 --> 00:08:10.660
The doctor was smiling,

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:12.093
but the news wasn't good.

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:15.490
Last verse.

00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:19.550
Darling Lorraine, please
don't leave me yet.

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:22.643
I know you're in pain,
pain you can't forget.

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:25.790
Maybe I'll go down to the corner store

00:08:25.790 --> 00:08:27.900
and get us something sweet.

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:31.263
Here's an extra blanket, honey,
to wrap around your feet.

00:08:32.790 --> 00:08:36.456
All the leaves were
washed with April rain.

00:08:36.456 --> 00:08:38.960
April is the cruelest month.

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:42.010
All the leaves were
washed with April rain,

00:08:42.010 --> 00:08:45.163
and the moon in the meadow
took Darling Lorraine.

00:08:46.851 --> 00:08:48.770
And that was that song.

00:08:48.770 --> 00:08:49.833
I didn't know.

00:08:49.833 --> 00:08:53.083
(audience applauding)

00:08:58.949 --> 00:09:01.532
(light music)

